<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Molise</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Molise is a region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, with its capital at Campobasso. In antiquity its mountains were the heart of the Samnites, while the Frentani held its coastal hills. Long part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, it was joined to Abruzzo after Italian unification and became a separate region in 1963, the second smallest in Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/molise.jpg" alt="Molise" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Molise_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Molise covers 4,460.65 square kilometres, making it the second smallest region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta-valley">Aosta Valley</a>. It is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a> to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a> to the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> to the south, and has 35 kilometres of sandy coastline on the Adriatic Sea. The countryside is mostly mountainous, with 55 percent covered by mountains and most of the rest by hills. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volturno">Volturno</a>, the longest river of southern Italy, rises there. The name first appears in the High Middle Ages as that of a Norman county, taken from the name of the ruling family, the de' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulins">Moulins</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Evidence from the site of Isernia La Pineta shows that Molise was inhabited in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a>, with traces of human presence up to 700 000 years old. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> arrived it formed part of Samnium. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentri">Pentri</a> settled around Isernia and Campobasso from about the 7th century BCE. Roman expansion from the 4th century BCE brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samnite-wars">Samnite Wars</a>. Isernia and Bojano, the Pentri capital, were captured, but the Samnites were completely defeated only after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> won the Social War.</p><p>The region declined in late antiquity, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> found it thinly populated. Around 667 Grimoald I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-the-lombards">King of the Lombards</a>, created the gastaldate of Bojano and gave it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> commander Alzeco. In 1045 Rudolf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulins">Moulins</a> conquered what became the County of Bojano, which broke up into smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> after the family lost power at the end of the 11th century. In 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a> extended the French provincial model to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and by 1811 the Province of Molise had roughly its present area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Molise has a population of 285,940 and its capital and largest city is Campobasso. Its two provinces are Campobasso and Isernia. It was formed in 1963 by separation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>, with which it had been joined as Abruzzi e Molise. Emigration in 1951–71 emptied many small towns. Its two minorities are the Molisan Croats, who speak an old Dalmatian dialect of Croatian, and speakers of Arbëresh, a dialect of Albanian. Its sights include the Italic theatre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietrabbondante">Pietrabbondante</a> and the Roman site of Saepinum at Altilia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Molise” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Frentani occupied the coastal hills of modern <b>Molise</b> and southern Abruzzo between the Sangro and the Fortore rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/frentani">Frentani</a></li><li><blockquote>The Samnites lived in the mountains of modern <b>Molise</b> and inland Campania.</blockquote><a href="/state/samnite-league">Samnite League</a></li></ul></section>
